  "knowledgePattern": "Intersect two integer families of trigonometric symmetry axes",
  "hints": [
    {
      "title": "Write the symmetry axes as families",
      "body": "<p>Use the peak and trough input values for each trig function, keeping an arbitrary integer in each family.</p>",
      "recap": "Vertical symmetry axes of sine and cosine repeat at half the full period, after accounting for the input phase and scale.",
      "pitfall": "Do not restrict the integer index to nonnegative values before the axes are located.",
      "pause": "Write the possible k-values separately for the two curves.",
      "recall": [
        {
          "lessonId": "p1-b3-l06",
          "reminder": "Vertical symmetry axes of sine and cosine repeat at half the full period, after accounting for the input phase and scale."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Impose the common-axis condition",
      "body": "<p>Equate the two families and use integer parity to determine which indices can coincide.</p>",
      "recap": "Parity and integer remainders constrain which integer parameters satisfy a relation.",
      "pitfall": "The first positive axis of one graph need not be an axis of the other.",
      "pause": "Describe all common axes, then locate the least positive one.",
      "recall": [
        {
          "lessonId": "p2-b3-l03",
          "reminder": "Parity and integer remainders constrain which integer parameters satisfy a relation."
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "solution": "<p><strong>1.</strong> For sine, vertical symmetry axes occur when its input is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>n</mi><mi>&#x003C0;</mi></mrow></math>. Thus <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>n</mi><mi>&#x003C0;</mi></mrow></math>, or <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>5</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>12</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>n</mi><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>.</p><p><strong>2.</strong> For cosine, its input must be <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi><mi>&#x003C0;</mi></mrow></math>, giving <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>12</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>m</mi><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></math>. Equating these and multiplying by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>12</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>&#x003C0;</mi></mrow></math> gives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>6</mn><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>4</mn><mi>m</mi></mrow></math>, hence <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>3</mn><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>m</mi></mrow></math>.</p><p><strong>3.</strong> This requires n odd; write <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>. Then <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>11</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>12</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>t</mi><mi>&#x003C0;</mi></mrow></math>. All integer t are allowed. The least positive value is obtained at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>11</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>12</mn></mrow></math>. Answer E.</p>"
}
